\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The floor, the hypothesis it requires, and alignment as a minimum cut.} \textbf  {(A)}~Floor $\beta $ computed---not assumed---as the minimum edge weight over four finite contact graphs spanning twelve orders of magnitude, including an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor. Positivity is a property of finiteness and strict positivity of the weights, not of their scale. \textbf  {(B)}~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero, with every $\varepsilon $ in a ladder down to $10^{-9}$ eventually cleared. Truncating the same sequence to a finite system (blue, first ten stages) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires finiteness, and this chart is included so that the requirement is visible rather than tacit. \textbf  {(C)}~Alignment computed as a minimum residue over admissible cuts, against an independent brute-force enumeration of every bipartition, on forty randomly generated graphs. Points lie on the identity to machine precision. This is a check of the implementation against ground truth, not a restatement of the definition. \textbf  {(D)}~Floor surface over edge count and weight scale. The surface is positive everywhere on the domain and descends toward zero only as the scale does; increasing the edge count at fixed scale lowers the floor but never abolishes it, which is the content of the minimum over a finite set.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel1}{{1}{5}{\textbf {The floor, the hypothesis it requires, and alignment as a minimum cut.} \textbf {(A)}~Floor $\beta $ computed---not assumed---as the minimum edge weight over four finite contact graphs spanning twelve orders of magnitude, including an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor. Positivity is a property of finiteness and strict positivity of the weights, not of their scale. \textbf {(B)}~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero, with every $\varepsilon $ in a ladder down to $10^{-9}$ eventually cleared. Truncating the same sequence to a finite system (blue, first ten stages) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires finiteness, and this chart is included so that the requirement is visible rather than tacit. \textbf {(C)}~Alignment computed as a minimum residue over admissible cuts, against an independent brute-force enumeration of every bipartition, on forty randomly generated graphs. Points lie on the identity to machine precision. This is a check of the implementation against ground truth, not a restatement of the definition. \textbf {(D)}~Floor surface over edge count and weight scale. The surface is positive everywhere on the domain and descends toward zero only as the scale does; increasing the edge count at fixed scale lowers the floor but never abolishes it, which is the content of the minimum over a finite set}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel1@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][4][]5}{}{}{}}
