\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Probing is free, committing is floored, and a structure committed once is thereafter traversed at no cost.} \textbf  {(A)}~Residue deposited by a commitment against the measure of the region committed, over six decades of measure. The deposit is constant and sits at the floor (dashed): the cost of individuating a region does not depend on how improbable that region is. This is the first clause of the asymmetry and it is the clause that makes rarity irrelevant to cost. \textbf  {(B)}~The commitment counter $M$ under the two operations. Commits increment it one for one; probes leave it flat at zero however many are performed. The counter is the logical clock of the semantics, and the two lines are the operational content of the probe/commit distinction. \textbf  {(C)}~Reuse. A single commitment is performed, after which two thousand traversals are executed. $M$ rises once and then stays; cumulative residue rises once and then stays. The structure is not consumed by the traversals, and no energetic argument enters the computation---only the asymmetry. \textbf  {(D)}~Probe frequency against region measure, with commitment cost as the vertical coordinate and the constant-cost plane overlaid. Frequency tracks measure over four decades while cost does not vary at all: rarity affects how often a region is visited and not what visiting it costs.}}{7}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel2}{{2}{7}{\textbf {Probing is free, committing is floored, and a structure committed once is thereafter traversed at no cost.} \textbf {(A)}~Residue deposited by a commitment against the measure of the region committed, over six decades of measure. The deposit is constant and sits at the floor (dashed): the cost of individuating a region does not depend on how improbable that region is. This is the first clause of the asymmetry and it is the clause that makes rarity irrelevant to cost. \textbf {(B)}~The commitment counter $M$ under the two operations. Commits increment it one for one; probes leave it flat at zero however many are performed. The counter is the logical clock of the semantics, and the two lines are the operational content of the probe/commit distinction. \textbf {(C)}~Reuse. A single commitment is performed, after which two thousand traversals are executed. $M$ rises once and then stays; cumulative residue rises once and then stays. The structure is not consumed by the traversals, and no energetic argument enters the computation---only the asymmetry. \textbf {(D)}~Probe frequency against region measure, with commitment cost as the vertical coordinate and the constant-cost plane overlaid. Frequency tracks measure over four decades while cost does not vary at all: rarity affects how often a region is visited and not what visiting it costs}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel2@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][6][]7}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Composition is multiplicative, and the alternatives are excluded by comparison rather than by assertion.} \textbf  {(A)}~Predicted against simulated composite power for four candidate composition laws on five hundred randomly generated ladders. The multiplicative law lies on the identity; the additive law saturates and overshoots, the maximum law systematically underestimates, and the mean law fails badly. The comparison is the test: scoring only the favoured law would not have distinguished it from the others. \textbf  {(B)}~Mean absolute error by law over four thousand ladders, on a logarithmic axis. The multiplicative law is exact to machine precision; the alternatives err by $0.047$, $0.179$ and $0.463$ respectively. The gap spans sixteen orders of magnitude and is not a matter of fit quality. \textbf  {(C)}~Residual fraction against ladder length for four rung powers, on a logarithmic axis. Each trace is a straight line, which is the graphical content of the residual being a product $\DOTSB \prod@ \slimits@ _i(1-\pi _i)$ rather than a sum. \textbf  {(D)}~Composite power over two rung powers. The surface is $1-(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: it rises to unity along either edge and is symmetric under exchange, so order does not matter, and it is strictly below the additive prediction everywhere in the interior.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel3}{{3}{7}{\textbf {Composition is multiplicative, and the alternatives are excluded by comparison rather than by assertion.} \textbf {(A)}~Predicted against simulated composite power for four candidate composition laws on five hundred randomly generated ladders. The multiplicative law lies on the identity; the additive law saturates and overshoots, the maximum law systematically underestimates, and the mean law fails badly. The comparison is the test: scoring only the favoured law would not have distinguished it from the others. \textbf {(B)}~Mean absolute error by law over four thousand ladders, on a logarithmic axis. The multiplicative law is exact to machine precision; the alternatives err by $0.047$, $0.179$ and $0.463$ respectively. The gap spans sixteen orders of magnitude and is not a matter of fit quality. \textbf {(C)}~Residual fraction against ladder length for four rung powers, on a logarithmic axis. Each trace is a straight line, which is the graphical content of the residual being a product $\prod _i(1-\pi _i)$ rather than a sum. \textbf {(D)}~Composite power over two rung powers. The surface is $1-(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: it rises to unity along either edge and is symmetric under exchange, so order does not matter, and it is strictly below the additive prediction everywhere in the interior}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel3@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][6][]7}{}{}{}}
\citation{borel1909,cantelli1917,williams1991}
