\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Repetition has diminishing returns; the dichotomy between reaching and plateauing is a property of the tail.} \textbf  {(A)}~Composite power under repetition of a single rung, for four rung powers. Each curve rises strictly, tends to unity (dashed), and reaches it at no finite $n$. A ladder of near-duplicate rungs therefore cannot attain a demanding target however long it is made. \textbf  {(B)}~Marginal contribution of the $n$-th repetition, $\pi (1-\pi )^{n-1}$, on a logarithmic axis. The decline is geometric. The stronger the rung, the larger the first contribution and the faster the decay---so repetition is least useful precisely where each copy is individually most effective. \textbf  {(C)}~Residual gap against rungs applied for three power series, indexed from $i=2$ so that no rung is absolute and the dichotomy is exhibited by tail behaviour rather than by a degenerate first step. The divergent series $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ 1/i$ drives the gap steadily toward zero; the convergent series $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ 1/i^2$ and $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ 2^{-i}$ plateau at finite residual and do not improve thereafter. \textbf  {(D)}~Rungs required to reach a target, over target and rung power. The surface diverges as the target approaches unity and as the rung power approaches zero, and is low only in the corner where strong rungs meet modest targets. This is the cost model: what a target costs is set jointly by how demanding it is and by how much each step accomplishes.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel4}{{4}{9}{\textbf {Repetition has diminishing returns; the dichotomy between reaching and plateauing is a property of the tail.} \textbf {(A)}~Composite power under repetition of a single rung, for four rung powers. Each curve rises strictly, tends to unity (dashed), and reaches it at no finite $n$. A ladder of near-duplicate rungs therefore cannot attain a demanding target however long it is made. \textbf {(B)}~Marginal contribution of the $n$-th repetition, $\pi (1-\pi )^{n-1}$, on a logarithmic axis. The decline is geometric. The stronger the rung, the larger the first contribution and the faster the decay---so repetition is least useful precisely where each copy is individually most effective. \textbf {(C)}~Residual gap against rungs applied for three power series, indexed from $i=2$ so that no rung is absolute and the dichotomy is exhibited by tail behaviour rather than by a degenerate first step. The divergent series $\sum 1/i$ drives the gap steadily toward zero; the convergent series $\sum 1/i^2$ and $\sum 2^{-i}$ plateau at finite residual and do not improve thereafter. \textbf {(D)}~Rungs required to reach a target, over target and rung power. The surface diverges as the target approaches unity and as the rung power approaches zero, and is low only in the corner where strong rungs meet modest targets. This is the cost model: what a target costs is set jointly by how demanding it is and by how much each step accomplishes}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel4@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][8][]9}{}{}{}}
