\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Control lies at the strongest rung, not the weakest.} \textbf  {(A)}~Rung power and sensitivity for the four-rung ladder of the worked demonstration. The two orderings agree: rung~3, of highest power $0.55$, also carries the highest sensitivity $0.308$. The naive expectation---that the weakest rung is the bottleneck and therefore the one worth improving---predicts the opposite ordering and is wrong. \textbf  {(B)}~Sensitivity normalised by the ladder's residual fraction, against rung power, for nine hundred random ladders. Every point lies on the curve $1/(1-\pi _j)$, which is exact: sensitivity is $P/(1-\pi _j)$ with $P$ the residual, so the normalised quantity depends on that rung alone and increases strictly in its power. The normalisation is necessary because raw sensitivity also depends on the other rungs, which obscures the relationship across ladders of differing composition. \textbf  {(C)}~Gain in composite power from a fixed improvement applied to each rung in turn, at three improvement sizes. Rung~3 returns the most at every size and rung~4---the weakest---returns the least. Improving the bottleneck is the worse investment, by roughly a factor of two at $\delta = 0.1$. \textbf  {(D)}~Sensitivity to the third rung of a three-rung ladder, over the powers of the other two. The surface is $(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: sensitivity is transmission through the remaining rungs, and it is largest when those rungs are weak. What a marginal gain buys is set by what the rest of the ladder does with it, which is why the quantity being maximised is transmission and not slack.}}{12}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel5}{{5}{12}{\textbf {Control lies at the strongest rung, not the weakest.} \textbf {(A)}~Rung power and sensitivity for the four-rung ladder of the worked demonstration. The two orderings agree: rung~3, of highest power $0.55$, also carries the highest sensitivity $0.308$. The naive expectation---that the weakest rung is the bottleneck and therefore the one worth improving---predicts the opposite ordering and is wrong. \textbf {(B)}~Sensitivity normalised by the ladder's residual fraction, against rung power, for nine hundred random ladders. Every point lies on the curve $1/(1-\pi _j)$, which is exact: sensitivity is $P/(1-\pi _j)$ with $P$ the residual, so the normalised quantity depends on that rung alone and increases strictly in its power. The normalisation is necessary because raw sensitivity also depends on the other rungs, which obscures the relationship across ladders of differing composition. \textbf {(C)}~Gain in composite power from a fixed improvement applied to each rung in turn, at three improvement sizes. Rung~3 returns the most at every size and rung~4---the weakest---returns the least. Improving the bottleneck is the worse investment, by roughly a factor of two at $\delta = 0.1$. \textbf {(D)}~Sensitivity to the third rung of a three-rung ladder, over the powers of the other two. The surface is $(1-\pi _1)(1-\pi _2)$: sensitivity is transmission through the remaining rungs, and it is largest when those rungs are weak. What a marginal gain buys is set by what the rest of the ladder does with it, which is why the quantity being maximised is transmission and not slack}{figure.5}{}}
