\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Labels move nothing, powers move everything, and the static verdict agrees with execution.} \textbf  {(A)}~Composite power before and after relabelling the rungs, on four hundred random ladders. All points lie on the identity. The relabelling is performed on annotations external to the formalism, since a rung carries exactly one datum and has no identity field: a test that compared objects carrying names would be testing the names. \textbf  {(B)}~The control. The same observable under perturbation of a rung \emph  {power} rather than a label. The distribution of changes is entirely positive and well away from zero. Without this chart panel~A would be consistent with a constant observable and would carry no information; with it, panel~A is a statement about labels specifically. \textbf  {(C)}~Static reachability against execution on five thousand generated programs. Four thousand and seventeen were accepted statically and nine hundred and eighty-three rejected; in no case did the static verdict disagree with the executed outcome, marked by the cross at zero. Both verdicts occur in quantity, so the test could have failed in either direction---a suite in which every program was accepted would not have tested rejection. \textbf  {(D)}~The worked ladder in three dimensions: rung powers, sensitivities, and the gap trajectory that descends from unity to the residual $0.1386$ across the four commitments. Every quantity plotted is computed from the four declared powers, and nothing about what the rungs are enters the computation or could be recovered from it.}}{15}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel6}{{6}{15}{\textbf {Labels move nothing, powers move everything, and the static verdict agrees with execution.} \textbf {(A)}~Composite power before and after relabelling the rungs, on four hundred random ladders. All points lie on the identity. The relabelling is performed on annotations external to the formalism, since a rung carries exactly one datum and has no identity field: a test that compared objects carrying names would be testing the names. \textbf {(B)}~The control. The same observable under perturbation of a rung \emph {power} rather than a label. The distribution of changes is entirely positive and well away from zero. Without this chart panel~A would be consistent with a constant observable and would carry no information; with it, panel~A is a statement about labels specifically. \textbf {(C)}~Static reachability against execution on five thousand generated programs. Four thousand and seventeen were accepted statically and nine hundred and eighty-three rejected; in no case did the static verdict disagree with the executed outcome, marked by the cross at zero. Both verdicts occur in quantity, so the test could have failed in either direction---a suite in which every program was accepted would not have tested rejection. \textbf {(D)}~The worked ladder in three dimensions: rung powers, sensitivities, and the gap trajectory that descends from unity to the residual $0.1386$ across the four commitments. Every quantity plotted is computed from the four declared powers, and nothing about what the rungs are enters the computation or could be recovered from it}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:panel6@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][14][]15}{}{}{}}
